# Agent Behavior Instructions

> **How to use this file:**
> This is a template showing how to guide AI agents. Rename to `instructions.md` when you have actual guidelines.
> These instructions will be read by Claude Code during task execution to ensure aligned behavior.

## General Behavior

### Goals
- Complete assigned tasks autonomously with minimal human intervention
- Maintain code quality and architectural consistency
- Follow all coding standards and testing requirements
- Document decisions and progress in memory files
- Escalate blockers rather than work around them

### Working Style
- **Thorough:** Review existing code patterns before writing new code
- **Incremental:** Work in small, testable steps rather than large refactorings
- **Communicative:** Update progress.md frequently, explain decisions in code comments
- **Careful:** Run tests before committing, never push broken code
- **Respectful:** Follow existing code style even if you'd do it differently

## Code Quality Standards

### Testing
1. Write tests BEFORE implementing features (TDD when possible)
2. Minimum coverage: 85% for new code
3. Run full test suite before committing: `npm test`
4. Include unit tests, integration tests, and E2E tests
5. Tests should be deterministic - no flaky tests
6. Document test purpose with clear test names

### Code Style
- Follow ESLint configuration strictly (it will auto-fix issues)
- Use TypeScript strict mode, no `any` types
- Max line length: 100 characters
- Comment complex logic, but code should be self-documenting
- Use meaningful variable names (no single letters except loop counters)

### Performance
- Profile before optimizing (use Chrome DevTools, Node profiler)
- Database queries should use indexes (check EXPLAIN ANALYZE)
- Client-side rendering should be under 16ms per frame
- API responses should be < 200ms for 95th percentile

### Security
- Never commit secrets (API keys, passwords, tokens)
- Use environment variables for all configuration
- Sanitize all user input before database queries
- Use prepared statements / parameterized queries always
- Validate data types and ranges at API boundaries

## Naming Conventions

### Files and Directories
- Use kebab-case for directories: `user-authentication/`
- Use kebab-case for non-test files: `user-service.ts`
- Use `.test.ts` suffix for test files: `user-service.test.ts`
- Use `.types.ts` for type definitions: `user.types.ts`

### Variables and Functions
- Use camelCase for variables and functions: `getUserById`, `isValidEmail`
- Use PascalCase for classes and React components: `UserService`, `UserCard`
- Use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for constants: `MAX_RETRIES = 3`
- Prefix boolean variables with `is`, `has`, `should`: `isLoading`, `hasError`

### Database
- Use snake_case for table names: `user_accounts`
- Use snake_case for column names: `created_at`, `user_id`
- Always have `id` as primary key
- Use `created_at` and `updated_at` timestamps on all tables

## Commit Messages

### Format
Use [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/):

```
type(scope): description

Optional body explaining why this change was needed.

Resolves: #123
Co-authored-by: Agent <agent@foundry.local>
```

### Types
- `feat` - New feature
- `fix` - Bug fix
- `docs` - Documentation changes
- `style` - Code style changes (formatting, semicolons, etc.)
- `refactor` - Code refactoring without feature changes
- `perf` - Performance improvements
- `test` - Adding or updating tests
- `chore` - Maintenance, dependencies, etc.

### Scope
- Specify the affected module: `api`, `frontend`, `auth`, `database`, etc.
- Examples: `feat(auth): add JWT refresh token logic`

### Examples
```
feat(api): add user pagination to /users endpoint

Add limit and offset query parameters to allow clients to paginate
through large user lists. Default limit is 50, max is 100.

Resolves: #456

fix(frontend): prevent button double-click submissions

Add disabled state while form submission is in progress.
Resolves: #789

test(api): add integration tests for auth middleware

Co-authored-by: Agent <agent@foundry.local>
```

## Working with Multiple Repositories

### Coordination
1. When a feature spans multiple repos, work in this order:
   - Shared types/interfaces first (`repos/shared/`)
   - Backend API changes (`repos/backend/`)
   - Frontend implementation (`repos/frontend/`)
   - Integration tests last

2. Create feature branches consistently across repos:
   - All: `feature/user-authentication`
   - All: `feature/api-pagination`

3. When changing shared types, verify backward compatibility

4. Test end-to-end after cross-repo changes

### Dependency Management
- Update package-lock.json after npm install
- Pin dependency versions explicitly
- Avoid major version upgrades without approval
- Check for security vulnerabilities: `npm audit`

## PR and Code Review Readiness

### Before Opening PR
- [ ] All tests passing locally: `npm test`
- [ ] No ESLint errors: `npm run lint`
- [ ] TypeScript type checking passes: `npx tsc --noEmit`
- [ ] Code formatted: `npm run format`
- [ ] Meaningful commit messages (conventional format)
- [ ] PR title follows conventional format: `feat: add pagination`
- [ ] PR description explains what and why

### PR Description Template
```markdown
## Description
Brief description of changes

## Related Issues
Resolves #123

## Type of Change
- [x] Feature
- [ ] Bug fix
- [ ] Breaking change
- [ ] Documentation

## Testing
Describe how to test these changes

## Checklist
- [x] Tests added/updated
- [x] Documentation updated
- [x] No breaking changes
- [x] Code follows style guide
```

## Error Handling

### Expected Behavior
- Catch all errors, never let them propagate silently
- Provide meaningful error messages with context
- Log errors with stack traces for debugging
- Graceful degradation when possible

### Examples
```typescript
// Bad - no error handling
const user = await db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = $1', [id]);

// Good - explicit error handling
try {
  const user = await db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = $1', [id]);
  if (!user) {
    throw new Error(`User not found: id=${id}`);
  }
  return user;
} catch (error) {
  logger.error('Failed to fetch user', { userId: id, error });
  throw new Error('Failed to fetch user from database');
}
```

## Documentation Requirements

### Code Comments
- Comment the WHY, not the WHAT
- Explain non-obvious logic
- Include examples for complex functions
- Mark TODO/FIXME with context

### Function Documentation
```typescript
/**
 * Calculates the recommended retry delay with exponential backoff.
 *
 * @param attempt - Current attempt number (0-based)
 * @param baseDelay - Base delay in milliseconds
 * @returns Delay in milliseconds, with random jitter
 *
 * @example
 * // First retry: ~1000ms
 * getRetryDelay(0, 1000);
 *
 * // Fifth retry: ~32000ms with jitter
 * getRetryDelay(4, 1000);
 */
function getRetryDelay(attempt: number, baseDelay: number): number {
  // Exponential backoff: 2^attempt * baseDelay
  const exponentialDelay = Math.pow(2, attempt) * baseDelay;
  // Add ±10% jitter to prevent thundering herd
  const jitter = exponentialDelay * (0.9 + Math.random() * 0.2);
  return Math.min(jitter, 30000); // Cap at 30 seconds
}
```

### README Updates
- Update relevant READMEs when adding new features
- Include setup instructions for new services
- Document new environment variables needed
- Add examples for new public APIs

## Handling Blockers

When stuck, follow this escalation:

1. **Investigate (5-10 minutes)**
   - Search codebase for similar patterns
   - Read error messages carefully
   - Check logs and debug output

2. **Document (5 minutes)**
   - Update `memory/blockers.md` with blocker details
   - Include error messages and what you've tried
   - Note any dependencies or context needed

3. **Escalate**
   - If blocker persists, stop work and signal human review
   - Include full context in progress update

## Resource Limits

### Work Session Guidelines
- Target: 2-4 hour focused work sessions
- Break down 8+ hour tasks into daily checkpoints
- Save progress frequently in memory files
- Commit working code regularly (don't accumulate changes)

### API and Rate Limits
- Claude API: 100 calls/hour (managed by Ralph)
- Database: Query timeouts are 30 seconds
- External APIs: Check rate limits, implement exponential backoff
- Retry failed requests up to 3 times with backoff

## Special Situations

### Working with Secrets
- Never log, print, or commit secrets
- Use environment variables, never config files
- .env files should be .gitignored
- For development, use .env.example with placeholder values

### Database Migrations
- Always write backward-compatible migrations
- Include rollback steps
- Test migration on a copy of production data
- Run migrations before deploying new code

### Infrastructure Changes
- Never modify infrastructure without documented approval
- Submit Terraform changes as PRs
- Test infrastructure changes in staging first
- Keep detailed changelogs in infrastructure/CHANGES.md

## Success Criteria for Tasks

A task is complete when:
1. Code is written and all tests pass
2. Code follows all standards in this document
3. All relevant READMEs and docs updated
4. Commit messages are meaningful
5. PR is created and ready for human review
6. Progress is documented in `memory/progress.md`

## When Uncertain

If unsure about approach, priority, or standards:
1. Check `context/coding-standards.md` for specific guidance
2. Check `context/architecture.md` for system design patterns
3. Review `memory/decisions.md` for similar precedents
4. Ask in PR comments rather than making assumptions
5. Default to conservative approach (more tests, fewer dependencies)
